First, what’s happening:

1. In the GIF you can see that once you drag a sprite out of its selector area, the Scratch‐GUI’s custom “duplicate/export/delete” context menu never comes back. Instead you get the browser’s native context menu.

2. Under the hood, the sprite item passes a prop `preventContextMenu={this.dragRecognizer.gestureInProgress()}` into the `<ContextMenuTrigger>`. That works the very first time (on right-click) because no drag is in progress, but after you finish dragging, **we never re-render** the component to pick up the now‐false `gestureInProgress()` flag. React still thinks `preventContextMenu === true`, so `<ContextMenuTrigger>` is permanently disabled and the OS’s context menu appears.

3. **Solution**: force a re-render in the container whenever drag starts and whenever drag ends, so that `preventContextMenu` flips to `true` during drag and back to `false` after drag.

Here are two small *SEARCH/REPLACE* edits to `src/containers/sprite-selector-item.jsx` to inject `this.forceUpdate()` in the right spots.

